1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Dr. Knud Knud-Hansen (1874-1951), left, an obstetrician, surgeon and general practitioner, visited the Danish West Indies in 1903 and settled there permanently in 1907. He established a public medical practice in t
2 CONC he same era as Dr. Viggo Christensen, and together they tirelessly provided critical medical care throughout the Danish West Indies. Knud-Hansen married the daughter of Dutch West Indians, Therese Louise “Ruby” Hassell, right, in 
2 CONC 1915. On the day the U.S. took over the islands in 1917, Knud-Hansen declared to his family, “I am now an American; we will not speak Danish again.” He stayed on after the transfer to help found the modern public health system in 
2 CONC the U.S. Virgin Islands and established a nurses training school. In 1953, the 116-bed Knud Knud-Hansen Memorial Hospital was opened and named posthumously in his honor. That same year, the Charles Harwood Memorial Hospital in Chr
2 CONC istiansted became operational with a modern 60-bed facility.
