1 OCCU General, Stortingsmann 1814, 1815-1817 for Hedmarkens amt, Oberst, Stortingsmann 1815-1817 for Hedmarkens amt, Oberst og Stortingsmann 1815-1817
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Han var representant i det første overordentlig Stortinget i 1814, og valgt for Hedmark amt til Stortinget i 1815-17.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT http://runeberg.org/halvforf/4/0651.html
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Johan Christopher Ræder (1782–1853)===
2 CONT
2 CONT Johan Christopher Ræder] (26 November 1782 – 16 July 1853 was a Norwegian military officer. He was of German and Danish descent, and partly served in the Danish army.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Personal life'''
2 CONT He was born in Copenhagen as a son of Johan Georg Ræder (1751–1808). The family soon moved to Romsdal in Norway, where he grew up. He was the great-grandson of Johan Georg Rhäder, the person who immigrated to Norway and started th
2 CONC e family branch there. He was also a brother of Nicolai Ditlev Amund Ræder, Johan Philip Thomas Ræder and Jacob Thode Ræder.
2 CONT
2 CONT In November 1810 in Halsa he married Kaja Munch (1794–1874), daughter of the Danish major Ole Munch. They had the children Jacques Ræder, Ole Munch Ræder, Nicolai Ditlev Ammon Ræder and Johan Georg Ræder. Through Nicolai he was th
2 CONC e paternal grandfather of Anton Henrik Ræder and Johan Christopher Ræder. Through his Danish brothers he was an uncle of Carl Gustav Valdemar Ræder, Johan Georg Frederik Ræder and Oscar Alexander Ræder and a granduncle of Hans Hen
2 CONC ning Ræder.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Career''' He took his military education in Copenhagen, and an engineer's examination in 1805. He served in the Royal Danish Army, from 1802 to 1805 in the Crown Prince Regiment, and from 1805 to 1809 in the Engineer Corps in R
2 CONC endsborg. He served in the General Command in Jutland from 1809 and in Norway from January 1810. He served as an engineer officer at Kongsvinger Fortress, and fought in the Swedish campaign against Norway (1814). He served in Kris
2 CONC tiania from 1815 to 1818, and in Trondhjem from 1818 to 1842. He was also manager of the engineer education at the Norwegian Military College from 1817 to 1818, and acting board member of the college from 1844 to 1845. He reached 
2 CONC the rank of Second Lieutenant in 1803, Captain in 1809/1810, Major in 1818, Lieutenant Colonel in 1828 and Colonel in 1842.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Norway's independence from Denmark in 1814 he experienced a personal crisis, since he was faced with giving up the allegiance to Denmark. He resigned from the Norwegian service, but retracted the resignation. He was even ele
2 CONC cted to the first session of the Parliament of Norway in 1814. He was later re-elected in 1815, representing the constituency of Hedemarkens Amt.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
