1 NAME John Ingolf Flemmer /Knud-Hansen/
2 GIVN John Ingolf Flemmer
2 SURN Knud-Hansen
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 3 MAR 1919
2 PLAC Charlotte Amalie, St. Thomas.
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 24 OCT 2012
2 PLAC Harleigh Lane in Oxford.
2 ADDR
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John Ingolf Flemmer Knud-Hansen, MD, USN, Ret. passed away Oct. 24, 2012 at his home on Harleigh Lane in Oxford. He was 93. He was among the most beloved and respected physicians on the Eastern Shore, a dedicated v
2 CONC eteran of the US Navy, and a loyal member of clubs and organizations that support health and human services.
2 CONT Born March 3, 1919 in Charlotte Amalie, St. Thomas, Dr. Knud-Hansen was the second son of Knud Knud-Hansen, MD, a Danish-born physician who visited the Danish West Indies in 1903, and decided to settle there permanently in 1907. H
2 CONC e arrived on board the Danish trawler, Ingolf, established a public medical practice with another physician, and together they provided nearly all the medical care for all three of the islands owned by Denmark. The first hospital 
2 CONC on St. Thomas was named the Knud Knud-Hansen Memorial Hospital.
2 CONT Dr. Knud-Hansen married the daughter of Dutch West Indians, Terese “Ruby” Hassell in 1915, and just as St. Thomas, St. John and St. Croix were being sold to the United States, his first son,James Angul Flemmer Knud-Hansen was born
2 CONC  Nov. 7, 1917…John Ingolf Flemmer Knud-Hansen was born March 3, 1919. Therefore, they both were born American citizens, and as such, his father proclaimed on the day the US took over the islands, “I am now an American; we will not
2 CONC  speak Danish again.”
2 CONT John I. F. Knud-Hansen was educated in St. Thomas and the British-run Boys Grammar School in Antigua until 1938, when he and his brother Jim earned scholarships to Swarthmore College in Pennsylvania. They both graduated in 1943; J
2 CONC im enlisted in the US Army and served as an infantryman in Sicilly and Palermo; John enlisted in the US Navy/Marines expedited medical school program, graduating from the University of Pennsylvania in 1945, and in uniform ready fo
2 CONC r service.
2 CONT Dr. John Knud-Hansen married Fredonia (Fritzi) Fulton Gephart, in her hometown of Pittsburgh, PA Feb. 3, 1945. A few months later, he was assigned to the Pacific Island of Saipan, where the US had established a Japanese POW camp. 
2 CONC The ugliness of war did not escape him, but he thrived in the Navy, making many friendships that lasted 50 or more years.
2 CONT After the war, Dr. Knud-Hansen embarked on a 21 year career with the U.S. Navy Medical Corps, in Philadelphia, Memphis, TN, Camp Pendleton in Oceanside, CA, Bethesda, MD, Boston, Annapolis and finally Charleston, SC. During that t
2 CONC ime, he and Fritzi raised four children, John, Erik , Chris and Emilie.
2 CONT He retired from active duty in 1964 and moved to Easton to establish a private practice as a general surgeon at the Memorial Hospital of Easton. At various times, he served as Chief of Staff and Chief of Surgery, as well as presid
2 CONC ent of the American Cancer Society of the Eastern Shore.
2 CONT On the recreational side, Dr. Knud-Hansen loved to read, and enjoy sports. He was an accomplished swimmer and tennis player in his earlier years, and was an ever-optimistic golfer, cursing the game on a regular basis. He was a pas
2 CONC t member and officer of the Talbot Country Club, a dedicated member of the Tred Avon Yacht Club, and a past Commodore of the Chesapeake Bay Yacht Club, where he was one of the most beloved (and cursed) poker and gin rummy players 
2 CONC in club history. He held a deep respect and affection for the employees of those clubs.
2 CONT Though not of Scottish blood, Dr. Knud-Hansen had a longtime enjoyment of Scottish country dancing, which he and his wife Fritzi taught locally, and spent several summers in St. Andrews, Scotland, to hone their dancing (and golfin
2 CONC g) skills. He was a past president of the local St. Andrews Society, and as a proud Norseman, was a founding member of the Leif Ericson Society International.
2 CONT Dr. Knud-Hansen also partnered with the late Douglas Hanks of Oxford by helping to underwrite the construction of Hanks’ crab skiff, The Slippery Eel. While never a boat owner, except a canoe named knu, Dr. Knud-Hansen was an avid
2 CONC  sailor who, over the years, sailed on several Newport-Bermuda races, Charleston, SC to Brazil race, and two trans-Atlantic races: 1966 Bermuda to Denmark and 1972 Bermuda to Spain. His primary crew responsibilities were doctor an
2 CONC d cook, at which he excelled. In his final years, he was happy to sit on the second floor deck at the TAYC watching Friday night sailing races.
2 CONT In 1991, Dr. Knud-Hansen’s wife of 46 years passed away after a battle with leukemia. During the long days of Fritzi’s hospitalization, Dr. Knud-Hansen was inspired to begin writing his memories of growing up in the Virgin Islands
2 CONC ….much like his father had written a book describing his decision to move From Denmark to the Virgin Islands. After his wife’s death, he visited the islands and wrote a few more chapters.
2 CONT In a marvelous twist of fate, he fell in love with Merrilyn Slack, herself widowed three years earlier when her husband of 46 years also died of leukemia. To the immense delight of all of their children, John and Merrilyn were mar
2 CONC ried in 1993 and enjoyed 19 wonderful years together. Both of them expressed pride in “being able to do marriage right!” It was Merrilyn who inspired Dr. Knud-Hansen to finish and publish Island Memories and Later Years in 1999.
2 CONT In addition to his beloved wife Merrilyn, Dr. Knud-Hansen is survived by his sons, John P. Knud-Hansen, MD of Easton, J. Erik Knud-Hansen of Hawaii, Christopher F. Knud-Hansen, Ph.D. of Boulder, CO and daughter Emilie, of Oxford; 
2 CONC and his Knud-Hansen grandchildren, Grant, Colin, Drew, Brent and Marissa, and Chelsea and Kayla Coleman; Mrs. Knud-Hansen’s family, The Slacks, also survive the doctor, Steve Slack, of Denver, Srimati Kamala of Bethesda, Susan Ski
2 CONC nner of Ellicott City and Sherri Koelbel of Denver, CO., and their spouses; plus many children, grandchildren; and one great-grand child. The mutual admiration between the Knud-Hansens and the Slacks has grown everlasting due to t
2 CONC he incredible love story of our parents.
2 CONT Also acknowledged with love and gratitude are the family home keeper Vicky Winstead of Denton and physical trainer Carrie Edelman of Royal Oak, lovingly contributing to the physical comfort and longevity for both Dr. & Mrs. Knud-H
2 CONC ansen. The family also wishes to thank Home Instead Senior Care for their compassionate and dedicated staff over such a brief period of time, and the Shore Home Health Care Hospice.
