2 CONT Browse Kristen's pictures by typing ''Feilberg'' here: https://www.rct.uk/collection/search#/page/1
2 CONT
2 CONT Kristen Feilberg or '''Christen Schjellerup Feilberg''' (1839–1919) was an early Danish photographer who is known mainly for his images captured far beyond the borders of Denmark. From the 1860s until the 1890s, Feilberg participa
2 CONC ted in expeditions to Sumatra, Singapore, and Penang. In 1867, he exhibited photos at the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exposition_Universelle_(1867) Paris World Exposition] and around 1870 he joined an expedition to the Batak la
2 CONC nds of East Sumatra with the Dutch explorer C. de Haan from which he returned with 45 successful "photogrammes".
2 CONT
2 CONT Kristen Feilberg was born on 26 August 1839 in Vester Vedsted near Ribe in the west of Jutland, Denmark. He was the son of Nikolai Laurentius Feilberg, a well-known cleric, and Conradine Antonette Caroline Købke. He was trained as
2 CONC  a photographer.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life in the East Indies'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Kristen Feilberg: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kristen_Feilberg#/media/File:Feilberg_Klings_Penang_1867.jpg Kling Indians in Penang.] Exhibited at Paris World Exposition in 1867. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After giving up his dream of becoming a painter like his uncle, the artist [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christen_K%C3%B8bke Christen Købke], he followed his sister [https://www.geni.com/people/Cecilie-Margrethe-Feilberg/60000000
2 CONC 09560986174?through=6000000008805353195 Cecilie Margrethe Feilberg] to Singapore in 1862. Here he worked partly as a tobacco agent, and became an owner of a plantation, along with his Danish colleague [https://www.geni.com/people/
2 CONC Nicolaj-Thomsen/6000000198947851834 Nicolaj Thomsen]. They also had some family relation. In addition, Kristen worked as a photographer. In 1864, Feilberg together with August Sachtler took over the photographic studio in Singapor
2 CONC e known as Sachtler & Co. Soon afterwards, together with E. Hermann Sachtler, he established a branch office in [https://www.google.com/maps/place/Penang,+Malaysia/@5.0598856,100.6899487,8.17z/data=!4m6!3m5!1s0x304a9403095390dd:0x
2 CONC 29c305a5f38f633f!8m2!3d5.2632341!4d100.4846227!16zL20vMGc2eHE?entry=ttu Penang]. In 1867, Feilberg set up his own studio in Penang and, the same year, exhibited 15 views of Penang and [https://www.google.com/maps/place/Sri+Lanka/@
2 CONC 7.857685,80.70625,7z/data=!3m1!4b1!4m6!3m5!1s0x3ae2593cf65a1e9d:0xe13da4b400e2d38c!8m2!3d7.873054!4d80.771797!16zL20vMDZtXzU?entry=ttu Ceylon] at the Paris World Exposition. He also produced a 10-part panoramic view of Penang take
2 CONC n from Edinburgh House. The earliest photographs of eastern [https://www.google.com/maps/place/Sumatra/@0.4757449,100.1997552,6.28z/data=!4m6!3m5!1s0x2e2aad6120570311:0x1def4d17e789deb4!8m2!3d-0.589724!4d101.3431058!16zL20vMDczNHI
2 CONC ?entry=ttu Sumatra] were taken by Feilberg in 1869. Considered to be of excellent quality, they include integrated group portraits of workers on tobacco plantations such as the one at Arendsburg. They are presented in three albums
2 CONC  entitled "Views" at the Royal Tropical Institute. In the late 1860s or early 1870s, Feilberg made a photographic tour of Sumatra. In 1867, he was already in Deli and he returned there in 1880. In September of that year, he joined
2 CONC  an expedition with C. de Haan who had been appointed by the Dutch East Indies government to explore the area in the interior around [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Toba Lake Toba] where he photographed the landscape and [http
2 CONC s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kristen_Feilberg#/media/File:Batak_Warriors_60011135_edit.jpg the Batak people], including the hierarchical princes. Photographing Lake Toba was an achievement of similar nature to the discovery of Lake V
2 CONC ictoria in 1858. Despite numerous obstacles, Feilberg was able to record the geography of the region, a feat highly appreciated by de Haan who spoke of the beauty of the landscape.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1880s, he again worked as a photographer in Singapore. He also worked as a buyer for the Danish East Asiatic Company. Some time after 1880, he spent a few years in Denmark where he also worked as a photographer. In 1890, he
2 CONC  returned to Singapore where he worked for several photo studios. He must also have taken part in an expedition to Borneo as evidenced by his photograph of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kristen_Feilberg#/media/File:Feilberg_Dyak_
2 CONC women_Borneo_1860s.jpg Dayak women] from the central area of the island. Scores of Feilberg's photographs from the collection at the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropenmuseum Tropenmuseum] in Amsterdam can be accessed on Wikimed
2 CONC ia Commons as well as at the Tropenmuseum itself. Feilberg died in Singapore in 1919.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family'''
2 CONT
2 CONT On May 1st 1865, Feilberg married Emma Alice Mac-Intine, a Scottish Indonesian with whom he had a daughter, Emma, in George Town, Penang. The mother died shortly after childbirth in March 1866. In 1876 on a visit to Denmark, Krist
2 CONC en married Anna Eleonora Sophie Lassen, and went back to Medan-Deli, Sumatra. They had one son, Hjalmar, who was born in 1877. In the beginning everything went fine, but somehow Kristen got mentally sick, which made him turn again
2 CONC st his colleague Nicolaj Thomsen and their plantation business. He also developed hallucinations with stronger Christian beliefs. Anna and Hjalmar returned to Denmark, while Kristen departed some months after. On the boat back hom
2 CONC e to Copenhagen he nearly committed suicide by jumping overboard, when it reached London. The Feilberg and Lassen-family fought for Kristen's wellbeing. It became a conflict. In Denmark Hjalmar died in 1880, at the age of 2 in Ege
2 CONC bjerg at Sorø. The marriage was never successful, but with their son's death, it had now reached a turning point. The Feilberg family never felt comfortable with Anna's personality. The couple separated in 1884 and Kristen moved b
2 CONC ack to Singapore for the third and last time in 1885 as a photographer. He was a broken man, and was never seen by the family again. He only existed through letters, but became the fascinating distant uncle in Asia.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/photo/view?album_type=photos_of_me&id=6000000008805353195&photo_id=6000000199401223823&position=0 Photo of Kristen and his adventurous nephew Albert Gottlieb jr. in Singapore, 1892.]
2 CONT
2 CONT In Singapore Kristen lived in 15 years until he in 1900 moved to Tringanu, 450 km north of Singapore. Here he had company by his three monkeys ''Kitty'', ''Jacho'' and ''Bambu Splinter'', a dog, three yellow cats, a white-tailed e
2 CONC agle on the balcony, a 3,5 meters long Boa Constrictor snake in a cage(!), a bear cub, and a tiger called [https://www.geni.com/photo/view/6000000008805353195?album_type=photos_of_me&photo_id=6000000199400967831 ''Tunku Fatima''] 
2 CONC (princess Fatima), awarded to him by the native hunters that called Kristen ''the Grandpa of the Country'' danish: ''Landets Bedstefader''. The tiger was eventually awarded to Zoologisk Have in Copenhagen. In the Feilberg family a
2 CONC  claw from the tiger is preserved in a golden necklace. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After he had been living in Tringanu (today [https://www.google.com/maps/place/Kuala+Terengganu,+Terengganu,+Malaysia/@5.3283755,103.0719291,11z/data=!4m15!1m8!3m7!1s0x31b6dd7557fbd357:0x6a8fe5afedf2a3fe!2sTerengganu,+Malaysia!3b1
2 CONC !8m2!3d5.0936342!4d102.989615!16zL20vMDIxMWpj!3m5!1s0x31b7099e976e862f:0x1bc0ee85cb232492!8m2!3d5.329624!4d103.1370142!16zL20vMDJ2bDBt?entry=ttu Kuala Terengganu]) as an agent for ''Øst Asiatisk Comp Ltd'', Kristen moved for the l
2 CONC ast time back to Singapore, and lived by a pretty fortune gained from his former workplace. He died in his home on [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dhoby_Ghaut 9. Dhoby Ghaut] in Singapore on the 12th of August 1919. His daughter Em
2 CONC ma, a nun in Japan, took care of him. 
